Hi Alexandre, hi all,

A media-worker follow-up to my standalone/APT update. The isolated-loss
fix did not cover genuine target changes: FFmpeg resets NVENC rate control
and forces an IDR, while our Intel paths reopen the codec. At low targets,
this could change static reference detail and cause repeated FIFO recovery.

Stock4.1 keeps the configured encoder settings and applies the network
budget before encoding. It pays for each complete encoded picture before
admitting another raw scanout, keeping the latest raw picture replaceable.
Under constraint this trades motion cadence for stable reference detail.
Explicit 90-kHz media timestamps preserve timing across skipped raw ticks.
The RTP/RTX pacer no longer needs the old hardware-overshoot allowance,
and fresh REMB bounds healthy upward recovery.

This is common VM/LXC worker code; the native bus/API proposal is unchanged.
New qualification includes NVENC H.264/HEVC decoded-pixel tests, Firefox
H.264 target changes and a shaped Chromium 20 -> 1 -> 20-Mbit/s connection.
The stream recovers to 60 fps. Abrupt capacity loss still causes pauses;
Intel/AMD hardware and interaction latency were not remeasured here.

The shared viewer also fixes stuck keys on in-page focus changes: it
releases held keys when video/keyboard input loses focus, captures keyup at
other elements and ignores DOM autorepeat from presses begun elsewhere.
Node and real Firefox/Chromium DOM tests verify the transmitted key edges.

The standalone preview is available as version 0.4.0+stock4.1. Full source,
matching debs, installation instructions and test evidence are linked at:
https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/releases

Signed APT repository:
https://rdmitry0911.github.io/qsm-rd/

The new source still needs native upstream review independently of this
distribution route. I would welcome feedback on the shared bus lifetime,
configuration and service/API boundary when you have time.

Thanks,
Dmitry
